{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md | sed -n '105,435p'\"","aggregated_output":"   105\t  makes it a refusal.\n   106\t\n   107\t**Contradictions, stated instead of designed around.**\n   108\t\n   109\t- **C1.** The signature cannot live in `m`: `datom.md` (line 168) fixes\n   110\t  `m` as an integer reference to a metadata entity.\n   111\t- **C2.** A head under a DHT key contradicts `dao.jing.dht.md` section 1.\n   112\t- **C3. A well-known name may not stand in for a stream's identity.**\n   113\t  `dao.stream.md` (Envelopes, lines 277-285) makes\n   114\t  `:dao.stream/identity` the identity of the sequence one transport\n   115\t  instance owns; `dao.stream.remote.md` 2.2 keeps that. A board is a new\n   116\t  ring in every process, so a fixed string used as its identity would\n   117\t  name a different logical stream after each restart, and the reflection\n   118\t  would report that string as its identity\n   119\t  (`src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:509-519`). Section 5.1 therefore\n   120\t  separates the two: a **name** is lookup data, resolved once to the\n   121\t  ring's real descriptor, and the reader then attaches normally. This\n   122\t  adds one request shape to `dao.stream.remote` and amends\n   123\t  `dao.stream.remote.md`; it does not amend `dao.stream.md`. Slice H2\n   124\t  carries it.\n   125\t- **C4. `yin.repl` already has the alias this design refuses.** It\n   126\t  serves two per-process rings under the fixed identities\n   127\t  `\"yin.repl/requests\"` and `\"yin.repl/answers\"`\n   128\t  (`src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:72-81`,\n   129\t  `src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:224-225`). That is the same\n   130\t  inconsistency, landed; it is not permission. It is outside this\n   131\t  design and is named in section 12 as a defect with its fix available:\n   132\t  the name resolution H2 adds.\n   133\t\n   134\t**Gaps.** A loaded index does not survive a restart (the node's\n   135\t`:loads` are process state). A name that moved under a live session is\n   136\tsilent. `announce!` is private and unsigned, correctly (5.4).\n   137\t\n   138\t## 5. The design\n   139\t\n   140\t### 5.1 Medium (decision 1)\n   141\t\n   142\t**The head trace lives on a ring the publisher alone writes. A reader\n   143\treaches it as a `dao.stream` reader handle. That is the whole coupling.**\n   144\t\n   145\t- The **head board** is a `dao.stream.ringbuffer` of capacity 1.\n   146\t- **The follower takes a reader handle per principal and calls only\n   147\t  `cursor` and `next` on it.** It does not know whether the handle is the\n   148\t  ring itself, a reflection over WebSocket, or anything else. Section 8\n   149\t  rests on this sentence.\n   150\t- **Exposure in the core: `dao.stream.ws`, on loopback.** The publisher's\n   151\t  node composes one WebSocket acceptor (`dao.stream.ws-project`) whose\n   152\t  mirror table has one entry, the board, surface `#{:reader}`, **under\n   153\t  the ring's own identity**, the random identity every ring is created\n   154\t  with. It listens on TCP at **the same port number as the node's UDP\n   155\t  socket**, path `/head`, so one `host:port` names both and the token\n   156\t  needs no second address.\n   157\t- **A name is looked up; it is never an identity (C3).** The **board\n   158\t  name** of a principal is `\"yin.head/\"` followed by the principal id\n   159\t  (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.2). It appears in no descriptor, no cursor\n   160\t  and no `:dao.stream/identity`.\n   161\t  - The serving peer's composition holds, beside its table, a **name\n   162\t    map** `{name identity}`, each value a key of its table.\n   163\t  - One request shape is added to `dao.stream.remote.md` 2.1. It carries\n   164\t    `:dao.stream.remote/name n` in place of `:dao.stream/identity`, with\n   165\t    op `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the only op a name may ride. The mirror\n   166\t    answers the `descriptor` answer of the entry the name maps to, with\n   167\t    that entry's true `:dao.stream/identity` and surface and the name\n   168\t    echoed. An unmapped name is the `not-found` error with the name. A\n   169\t    named request with any other op is malformed and dropped.\n   170\t  - The asking link gains `resolve`: it sends that request and answers\n   171\t    `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true` until the answer is\n   172\t    filed, as `cursor` does (2.4). Its `ok` carries the remote\n   173\t    descriptor of the real stream over the same channel.\n   174\t  - The reader's ws composition resolves, then calls `attach!` on the\n   175\t    descriptor answered. From there the contract is unamended: the\n   176\t    reflection's identity is the ring's, and its cursors are the ring's.\n   177\t  - A restarted publisher has a new ring and a new identity. The reader\n   178\t    learns it by resolving again after `:source-lost` (5.3). Two nodes\n   179\t    that share a key serve two rings with two identities; a reader's\n   180\t    one source reaches one of them and never confuses their cursors.\n   181\t- The board has its own endpoint. It is never entered in the REPL\n   182\t  service's table: that table carries `yin.repl/requests`, which is\n   183\t  evaluation.\n   184\t- **Loopback only.** The endpoint is composed only when the node's bind\n   185\t  host is a loopback literal. On any other bind the node serves no\n   186\t  board, prints no token and says so. Section 8 says what lifts this.\n   187\t- **One source per principal**: the address the reader was given. Nothing\n   188\t  is put in the DHT, which is used as today to fetch the blobs.\n   189\t\n   190\t**Availability.** Publisher running: the reader asks once per poll\n   191\tinterval and observes a new head at the first poll that is answered.\n   192\tPublisher stopped: the reader keeps the head it installed and everything\n   193\tit linked. Nothing times out. A reader that never reached its source has\n   194\tno head; 5.6 says what a `require` does then.\n   195\t\n   196\tRejected: UDP on the DHT socket as the first exposure (section 8.3); a\n   197\tDHT key (C2).\n   198\t\n   199\t### 5.2 Trace schema (decision 2)\n   200\t\n   201\t```clojure\n   202\t{:yin.head/envelope {:yin.head/principal \"ed25519:<64 lowercase hex>\"\n   203\t                     :yin.head/manifest  :segment/...\n   204\t                     :yin.head/seq       1042}\n   205\t :yin.head/proof    {:yin.head/signature \"<128 lowercase hex>\"}}\n   206\t```\n   207\t\n   208\t- The key sets are closed; a trace carrying another key is malformed.\n   209\t- No time and no address are written. A trace says nothing about where\n   210\t  it was read, which is what lets any transport, and later any relay,\n   211\t  carry it unchanged.\n   212\t- The signed message is\n   213\t  `UTF-8(\"yin.head/trace:v1\\n\") || dao.jing/canonical-bytes(envelope)`.\n   214\t  The prefix is domain separation from name envelopes\n   215\t  (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.3). The primitive is `yin.vm.linker.sign`'s\n   216\t  Ed25519, unchanged. The principal is its own key.\n   217\t- **The sequence is derived.** It is the greatest transaction `t` among\n   218\t  the datoms of the index the manifest names: a nonnegative integer, and\n   219\t  **0 is valid**, since the first transaction is `t = 0`\n   220\t  (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:117-131`). An index of no datoms\n   221\t  has no trace. The publisher stores no counter.\n   222\t- **A reader confirms it.** A loaded index whose greatest `t` is not the\n   223\t  trace's sequence refuses the trace, `:yin.head/seq-mismatch`.\n   224\t- **Proven for the REPL path, enforced for every caller.** That two HEAD\n   225\t  writes of one directory never share a greatest `t`, across restart and\n   226\t  `(reset)`, holds for `yin.repl.index` (`index.cljc:189-233, 378-435`)\n   227\t  and H0 pins it. `deposit!` takes the datoms, never a sequence, and\n   228\t  refuses within one process a sequence equal to the last it signed with\n   229\t  another manifest (`:yin.head/seq-collision`) or below it\n   230\t  (`:yin.head/seq-regression`).\n   231\t- **The key kept and the directory lost** restarts the sequence; readers\n   232\t  refuse the new traces as stale. The remedy is\n   233\t  `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.5's, unchanged and still available (5.8):\n   234\t  hydrate the directory from the last published manifest before\n   235\t  publishing, or take a new key.\n   236\t\n   237\tRejected: the `m` slot (C1); a stored counter; a `:prev` chain.\n   238\t\n   239\t### 5.3 Retention (decision 3)\n   240\t\n   241\t**Capacity 1. The latest head wins and the ring coalesces.**\n   242\t\n   243\t- **First contact.** The reader mints `:dao.stream/oldest`; on a\n   244\t  capacity-1 ring that is the current head. An empty ring is `blocked`.\n   245\t- **A slow reader** is answered `gap` with the recovery cursor, the\n   246\t  current head's position, and adopts it.\n   247\t- **Anything else is a lost source.** On `cursor-mismatch`, `end`,\n   248\t  `not-found`, or a `transport-error` that is not retryable, the\n   249\t  follower drops the handle and its cursor and emits `:source-lost` with\n   250\t  the outcome. Its owner attaches a fresh handle; the follower mints\n   251\t  `:oldest` on it. A restarted publisher is this case, whatever the new\n   252\t  ring's identity is and however the transport reports it.\n   253\t\n   254\t### 5.4 Publisher behaviour (decision 4)\n   255\t\n   256\t- One trace is deposited at every HEAD move: after the HEAD write, in\n   257\t  the same call, before any network result, as HEAD itself moves\n   258\t  (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.5.6). The deposit is a local append.\n   259\t- The publisher does not batch; the ring does.\n   260\t- At startup a node with a key and a recovered HEAD deposits that HEAD's\n   261\t  trace, so a reader's first contact needs no new round.\n   262\t- A node deposits when it has a key, a socket and `:publish?`.\n   263\t- **`announce!` and the ledger ring stay as they are, beside the\n   264\t  board.** The ledger ring is the node's private work queue of put\n   265\t  addresses; `dao.space.dht` holds no key and knows nothing of `yin.*`.\n   266\t  The composition that calls `announce!` calls `deposit!` after it.\n   267\t\n   268\t### 5.5 Reader behaviour (decision 5)\n   269\t\n   270\t**Following** is composition: `--dht-follow <64 hex>@<host:port>`, or\n   271\t`:follow` to the plain API. A followed principal must be declared\n   272\t(`--dht-principal`); otherwise startup is refused.\n   273\t\n   274\t**Per principal the follower keeps three things, each with one job.**\n   275\t\n   276\t<table>\n   277\t<tr><th>Thing</th><th>What it is</th><th>What it decides</th></tr>\n   278\t<tr><td>The <strong>floor</strong></td>\n   279\t<td>The installed head's sequence, or <code>nil</code>: none was ever\n   280\tinstalled. Read from the persisted trace (5.7).</td>\n   281\t<td>Replay and rollback, and nothing else.</td></tr>\n   282\t<tr><td>The <strong>candidate</strong></td>\n   283\t<td>At most one verified trace above the floor. Process state.</td>\n   284\t<td>What is loading. It proves nothing and raises nothing.</td></tr>\n   285\t<tr><td>The <strong>installed head</strong></td>\n   286\t<td>One trace: verified, loaded, confirmed by its index, persisted.</td>\n   287\t<td>The snapshot set and the floor.</td></tr>\n   288\t</table>\n   289\t\n   290\t**The rule for one observed trace** is `judge`, a pure function of the\n   291\tfollowed principal, the floor, the installed manifest and the trace:\n   292\t\n   293\t<table>\n   294\t<tr><th>Case</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>\n   295\t<tr><td>Not the closed shape of 5.2, or a sequence that is not a\n   296\tnonnegative integer</td>\n   297\t<td><code>:yin.head/malformed</code></td></tr>\n   298\t<tr><td>Principal is not the followed one</td>\n   299\t<td><code>:yin.head/wrong-principal</code></td></tr>\n   300\t<tr><td>Signature does not verify</td>\n   301\t<td><code>:yin.head/bad-proof</code></td></tr>\n   302\t<tr><td>The floor is <code>nil</code></td>\n   303\t<td><code>:candidate</code>, whatever the sequence, 0 included</td></tr>\n   304\t<tr><td>Sequence below the floor</td>\n   305\t<td><code>:yin.head/stale</code></td></tr>\n   306\t<tr><td>Sequence at the floor, the installed manifest</td>\n   307\t<td><code>:duplicate</code></td></tr>\n   308\t<tr><td>Sequence at the floor, another manifest</td>\n   309\t<td><code>:yin.head/equivocation</code></td></tr>\n   310\t<tr><td>Sequence above the floor</td>\n   311\t<td><code>:candidate</code></td></tr>\n   312\t</table>\n   313\t\n   314\t- **`judge` has no source argument and no candidate argument.** Its\n   315\t  answer for a trace is the same whoever carried it, in whatever order,\n   316\t  and whatever is loading. One source is the degenerate case of many.\n   317\t- **The floor is confirmed evidence only.** It moves at installation\n   318\t  and at no other time. A candidate's sequence is a claim and is never\n   319\t  compared against a later trace.\n   320\t\n   321\t**The candidate: latest observed wins.**\n   322\t\n   323\t- A trace judged `:candidate` **replaces** the current candidate, unless\n   324\t  it is the same trace or the one last rejected (below). It replaces a\n   325\t  candidate of a higher sequence too: the source's newest element is its\n   326\t  current claim, and one slot has no capacity to exhaust.\n   327\t- **A replaced candidate is released at once, and the new one starts\n   328\t  loading in the same step.** The follower does not wait for the old\n   329\t  load. So a newer head never waits on an older one, and no deadline is\n   330\t  added.\n   331\t- The candidate's load is `dao.space.dht/load` with the covered-index\n   332\t  walk under the kind `:yin.head/candidate`. `loaded-indexes` lists only\n   333\t  index-kind loads (`dao/space/dht.cljc:1214-1227`), so **a candidate is\n   334\t  in no snapshot set and no name resolves through it**, with no change\n   335\t  to that function.\n   336\t- **Candidate records have owners.** Loads are keyed by address\n   337\t  (`dao/space/dht.cljc:1166-1170`) and two followed principals may name\n   338\t  one manifest, so the follower keeps, for the candidate-kind records\n   339\t  it started, a map `{address #{principal}}`. It is follower state; the\n   340\t  node learns nothing of it.\n   341\t  - A principal whose candidate names an address **acquires** it: the\n   342\t    principal joins the set, and the load is started if no record\n   343\t    exists.\n   344\t  - A principal **releases** an address when its candidate is replaced,\n   345\t    when it is rejected, and **when it is installed**. Release removes\n   346\t    the principal from the set. Only when the set is empty is the\n   347\t    record removed: abandoned if `:loading`, forgotten if terminal.\n   348\t  - So one principal's replacement or rejection never touches a load\n   349\t    another principal still wants, and a sequence mismatch is one\n   350\t    principal's verdict on its own trace, not on the record.\n   351\t  - Release at installation is the cleanup that makes a later\n   352\t    `load-index` by hand succeed: the candidate record is gone, the\n   353\t    blobs are local, and the index-kind load completes without a fetch.\n   354\t    The installed head does not need the record; the snapshot set reads\n   355\t    the local store.\n   356\t- **Kinds do not mix (D3).** `load` of an address already recorded under\n   357\t  another kind is refused, `:dao.space.dht/kind-conflict`, instead of\n   358\t  silently left. If the candidate's manifest is already loaded by hand\n   359\t  (index kind), the follower starts nothing, owns nothing, and reads\n   360\t  that record; it never forgets or abandons a record that is not of the\n   361\t  candidate kind. A `load-index` by hand of a manifest that is loading\n   362\t  as a candidate is refused with that code as data (H1 carries the\n   363\t  host module's translation), and succeeds once the head is installed.\n   364\t- **`abandon` leaves nothing behind.**\n   365\t  `(dao.space.dht/abandon node address)` removes a `:loading` record,\n   366\t  emits no event, and retires that record's interest in the fetch\n   367\t  client: the retained unsent request, if it is this record's, by\n   368\t  `dao.jing.content.step/abandon`; otherwise its outstanding id by\n   369\t  `dao.jing.content.step/retire`, after which a late answer is\n   370\t  unsolicited and dropped. Both functions exist. Each load has its own\n   371\t  request id, so another load fetching the same address is untouched.\n   372\t  `advance-loads` today steps the client only while a record is\n   373\t  `:loading` (`dao/space/dht.cljc:1313-1321`); it changes to step it\n   374\t  whenever a record is loading **or the client holds an unsent request,\n   375\t  an outstanding id or an undelivered completion**, so what an abandon\n   376\t  leaves is drained with no load active. What remains outside the node\n   377\t  is one get inside the DHT, which ends by the DHT's own `get-ticks`\n   378\t  and whose answer nobody claims.\n   379\t- **Confirmed.** The load is `:loaded` and the greatest `t` of its\n   380\t  datoms equals the sequence. The follower emits `:confirmed` with the\n   381\t  trace and changes nothing else (5.7).\n   382\t- **Rejected.** The load is `:loaded` and the check fails:\n   383\t  `:yin.head/seq-mismatch`. The candidate is dropped, its address\n   384\t  released, and its envelope id kept as the principal's **last\n   385\t  rejected**, one value, so the same trace shown at every poll is not\n   386\t  loaded again. The floor is where it was. Any other candidate trace\n   387\t  takes the slot as usual. One value remembers one trace: two wrong\n   388\t  traces shown in alternation are each walked again each time. After\n   389\t  the first time their blobs are local, so that is a local walk, not a\n   390\t  fetch.\n   391\t- **What one slot does not give: progress under replay.** Every signed\n   392\t  trace above the floor stays eligible. A source, or whoever sits on a\n   393\t  cleartext channel to it, that alternates two eligible traces makes the\n   394\t  reader release one load and start the other at each turn. Fetched\n   395\t  blobs stay local, so a trace shown long enough to fetch one blob makes\n   396\t  progress, and a true head whose load completes is installed and raises\n   397\t  the floor past the older ones. But if no load completes within a turn,\n   398\t  no head is installed for as long as the alternation lasts. The\n   399\t  signature gives integrity and rollback protection; it does not give\n   400\t  liveness. The work this can cause is bounded: one load per principal\n   401\t  in the node, and in the DHT at most one orphaned get per turn, each\n   402\t  ended by `get-ticks` and all under the DHT's pending-get bound. A\n   403\t  stronger guarantee needs an authenticated channel or a reviewed\n   404\t  scheduling policy, and both are section 12's.\n   405\t- **Unloadable.** The load failed: `:yin.head/unloadable` with the\n   406\t  failure as data. The candidate stays. After a delay in node ticks,\n   407\t  doubling from `:repair-ticks` to `:repair-max-ticks`, the failed\n   408\t  record is forgotten and the load started again **under the kind the\n   409\t  record had**, so a failed load made by hand at that address is\n   410\t  restarted as an index load and stays the user's. No deadline ends the\n   411\t  retries; a newer observed trace replaces the candidate.\n   412\t\n   413\t**The snapshot set** (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 7.2, amended) is: the\n   414\tmanifest the directory's HEAD names; every index-kind load that is\n   415\t`:loaded`; and **the installed head of each followed principal**, read\n   416\tfrom the node's local store, where every blob of it already is. The\n   417\tfollower records the installed manifests on the node value;\n   418\t`yin.vm.linker.dht/snapshots` reads them. Resolution (7.3) is otherwise\n   419\tunchanged.\n   420\t\n   421\t- The reader's own HEAD is never written by following.\n   422\t- Installation replaces the principal's installed manifest in one step.\n   423\t  No fold sees two heads of one principal, or none.\n   424\t\n   425\t**A silent publisher** changes nothing. The follower records the node\n   426\ttick reading at which its source last answered, as data for a drive\n   427\tthat wants to judge liveness with `dao.lease`. Nothing here reads it.\n   428\t\n   429\t### 5.6 When `(require 'alib)` refreshes (decision 6)\n   430\t\n   431\t**On a head event.** A require never asks the network for a newer head.\n   432\t\n   433\t- A `require` that links resolves against the snapshot set as it stands.\n   434\t- **A name may change meaning between two requires**: in two sessions,\n   435\t  or before and after `(reset)`. The require's provenance carries the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
