{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc | sed -n '708,755p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc | sed -n '1260,1330p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md | sed -n '620,900p'\"","aggregated_output":"   708\t(defn retire\n   709\t  \"Give up on the outstanding request `id` with `reason` (the blocking\n   710\t   driver's deadline or interruption): the id leaves :outstanding, every\n   711\t   route claiming one of its record's ids dies -- so any late answer is\n   712\t   dropped as unsolicited -- and a materialization record the id still\n   713\t   claims is completed :lost with it, so exactly one completion per\n   714\t   materialization survives the retirement.\"\n   715\t  ([state id] (retire state id abandoned-code))\n   716\t  ([state id reason]\n   717\t   (let [put-id (get (:routes state) id)\n   718\t         record (get (:materializations state) put-id)]\n   719\t     (if record\n   720\t       (let [claimed (remove nil? [put-id (get record :get-id)])]\n   721\t         (-> (reduce (fn [s i]\n   722\t                       (-> s\n   723\t                           (update :outstanding dissoc i)\n   724\t                           (update :routes dissoc i)))\n   725\t                     state\n   726\t                     claimed)\n   727\t             (update :completed\n   728\t                     conj (merge (materialization-entry record)\n   729\t                                 {:lost reason}))\n   730\t             (update :materializations dissoc put-id)))\n   731\t       (-> state\n   732\t           (update :outstanding dissoc id)\n   733\t           (update :routes dissoc id))))))\n   734\t\n   735\t\n   736\t(defn abandon\n   737\t  \"Give up on the retained unsent request with `reason` (or this\n   738\t   layer's abandoned-code): the composition that is about to change what\n   739\t   the writer is -- an operator disconnect, a rebind onto a fresh\n   740\t   attachment -- owns the decision that the retained request no longer\n   741\t   belongs to the new binding. The loss is appended as an ordinary\n   742\t   completion and surfaces at the next step's drain, routed by its id.\n   743\t   Records with nothing on the wire are untouched: abandon retires the\n   744\t   one delivery this binding still owes, not the medium's in-flight\n   745\t   answers.\"\n   746\t  ([state] (abandon state abandoned-code))\n   747\t  ([state reason]\n   748\t   (if-some [unsent (:unsent state)]\n   749\t     (-> state\n   750\t         (update :completed conj {:id (:id unsent),\n   751\t                                  :request (dissoc (:value unsent)\n   752\t                                                   :jing/request),\n   753\t                                  :lost reason})\n   754\t         (assoc :unsent nil))\n   755\t     state)))\n  1260\t\n  1261\t(defn- advance-load\n  1262\t  [node m done-by-id]\n  1263\t  (let [{:keys [fetching kind walk], :as record} (get-in node [:loads m])\n  1264\t        done (when fetching (get done-by-id (:id fetching)))]\n  1265\t    (cond\n  1266\t      (and fetching (nil? done)) [node []]\n  1267\t\n  1268\t      ;; the miss cause stays: another load may wait on the same address;\n  1269\t      ;; advance-loads drops it once none does\n  1270\t      (and done (not (:found? done)))\n  1271\t      (let [a (:address fetching)]\n  1272\t        (fail-load node m {::failure :miss :address a\n  1273\t                           :cause (get-in node [:misses a] ::dht/gap)}))\n  1274\t\n  1275\t      :else\n  1276\t      (let [fetched (cond-> (:fetched record) done inc)\n  1277\t            node (update-in node [:loads m] assoc :fetching nil :fetched\n  1278\t                            fetched)\n  1279\t            walked (walk-outcome walk (local node))]\n  1280\t        (case (::walk walked)\n  1281\t          :complete\n  1282\t          [(update-in node [:loads m] #(-> % (assoc :status :loaded :value\n  1283\t                                                    (:value walked))\n  1284\t                                           (dissoc :walk)))\n  1285\t           [(cond-> {::event :loaded :manifest m :kind kind :fetched fetched}\n  1286\t              (= index-kind kind) (assoc :datoms (count (:value walked))))]]\n  1287\t\n  1288\t          :invalid\n  1289\t          (fail-load node m {::failure :invalid :address (:address walked)\n  1290\t                             :defect (:defect walked)})\n  1291\t\n  1292\t          :missing\n  1293\t          (let [a (:address walked)\n  1294\t                {:keys [outcome id], :as asked} (content.step/request-get\n  1295\t                                                  (:client node) a)]\n  1296\t            (case outcome\n  1297\t              (:requested :pending-request)\n  1298\t              [(-> node\n  1299\t                   (assoc :client (:state asked))\n  1300\t                   (assoc-in [:loads m :fetching] {:id id :address a}))\n  1301\t               []]\n  1302\t\n  1303\t              ;; the client still owes a request: ask again next step\n  1304\t              :busy [(assoc node :client (:state asked)) []]\n  1305\t\n  1306\t              (fail-load (assoc node :client (:state asked)) m\n  1307\t                         {::failure :unaskable :address a :outcome\n  1308\t                          outcome}))))))))\n  1309\t\n  1310\t\n  1311\t(defn- advance-loads\n  1312\t  [node]\n  1313\t  (let [loading (sort-by str (keep (fn [[m r]]\n  1314\t                                     (when (= :loading (:status r))\n  1315\t                                       m))\n  1316\t                                   (:loads node)))]\n  1317\t    (if (or (empty? loading) (nil? (:dht node)))\n  1318\t      [node []]\n  1319\t      (let [{:keys [state completions]} (content.step/step (:client node)\n  1320\t                                                           fetch-budget)\n  1321\t            done-by-id (into {} (map (juxt :id identity)) completions)\n  1322\t            [node events] (reduce (fn [[node events] m]\n  1323\t                                    (let [[node more] (advance-load node m\n  1324\t                                                                    done-by-id)]\n  1325\t                                      [node (into events more)]))\n  1326\t                                  [(assoc node :client state) []]\n  1327\t                                  loading)]\n  1328\t        ;; every load waiting on an address has read its miss cause: keep\n  1329\t        ;; only the causes of addresses still being fetched\n  1330\t        [(update node :misses select-keys (fetching node)) events]))))\n   620\t\n   621\t## 7. Limits and defaults\n   622\t\n   623\t<table>\n   624\t<tr><th>Datum</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n   625\t<tr><td>Board capacity</td><td>1</td><td>Fixed.</td></tr>\n   626\t<tr><td>Candidates per principal</td><td>1</td><td>Fixed (5.5).</td></tr>\n   627\t<tr><td><code>:head-poll-ticks</code></td><td>5000</td>\n   628\t<td>Node ticks between two <code>next</code> calls on one source. It\n   629\tbounds how often a reader asks, not how late it learns.</td></tr>\n   630\t<tr><td>Retry delays (load, write, re-dial)</td>\n   631\t<td><code>:repair-ticks</code> to <code>:repair-max-ticks</code></td>\n   632\t<td>Doubling, the node's existing bounds.</td></tr>\n   633\t<tr><td>Followed principals</td><td>at most 64</td>\n   634\t<td>More refuses startup.</td></tr>\n   635\t<tr><td>Board endpoint slots</td><td>8</td>\n   636\t<td>The acceptor's handoff slots, as the REPL endpoint's.</td></tr>\n   637\t</table>\n   638\t\n   639\tFollowing never makes the node `busy?`.\n   640\t\n   641\t## 8. Cross-machine: the owner's question\n   642\t\n   643\t> if the abstraction is dao.stream, cross-machine would not require a\n   644\t> complete redesign, right?\n   645\t\n   646\t**Right.** The core is built so that it is true, and the one place it\n   647\twould have been false is removed. Following across machines changes how\n   648\ta reader handle is obtained and who may connect to the board. It changes\n   649\tno trace, no rule, no file and no reader semantics.\n   650\t\n   651\t### 8.1 What is transport-agnostic, and stays so\n   652\t\n   653\t<table>\n   654\t<tr><th>Part</th><th>Why it cannot depend on a transport</th></tr>\n   655\t<tr><td>The trace and its signature (5.2)</td>\n   656\t<td>No address, no time, no channel datum is signed.</td></tr>\n   657\t<tr><td><code>judge</code> (5.5)</td>\n   658\t<td>No source argument. One source is the degenerate case.</td></tr>\n   659\t<tr><td>Confirmation (5.2, 5.5)</td>\n   660\t<td>The index is content-addressed and fetched by the DHT, which\n   661\tverifies every blob against its address.</td></tr>\n   662\t<tr><td>Persistence (5.7)</td>\n   663\t<td><code>heads.edn</code> holds signed traces and no source.</td></tr>\n   664\t<tr><td>The follower (5.3, 5.5)</td>\n   665\t<td>It holds a reader handle and calls <code>cursor</code> and\n   666\t<code>next</code>. A lost source is one event, whatever lost it.</td>\n   667\t</tr>\n   668\t<tr><td>Reader semantics (5.6, 5.7)</td>\n   669\t<td>They start at the snapshot set.</td></tr>\n   670\t</table>\n   671\t\n   672\tOnly `yin.vm.linker.head.ws` and the shell's flags know a transport.\n   673\tSlice H1 proves the claim by test: the follower is driven to completion\n   674\tover a plain local ring, with no channel at all.\n   675\t\n   676\t### 8.2 What each later step adds and amends\n   677\t\n   678\t<table>\n   679\t<tr><th>Step</th><th>Adds</th><th>Amends</th>\n   680\t<th>Changes the core's trace, rule or persistence?</th></tr>\n   681\t<tr><td><strong>Serving off loopback</strong> (8.3)</td>\n   682\t<td>A bind that is not loopback; a bound on concurrent sessions; a\n   683\tbanner line.</td>\n   684\t<td>This document 5.1 and 5.8; <code>yin.vm.linker.head.ws</code>;\n   685\t<code>yin.repl.main</code>. Nothing in <code>dao.stream.*</code> if the\n   686\tunverified items of 8.3 hold.</td>\n   687\t<td><strong>No.</strong></td></tr>\n   688\t<tr><td><strong>Relaying</strong></td>\n   689\t<td>A second ring per followed principal holding the installed trace\n   690\tverbatim, read from <code>heads.edn</code>; a flag.</td>\n   691\t<td>Nothing in <code>dao.stream.*</code>: name resolution is in the\n   692\tcore (5.1), and a relay is one more peer mapping the same name to its\n   693\town ring. This document 5.1 and 5.5; the shell.</td>\n   694\t<td><strong>No.</strong> It is why the trace carries no address and\n   695\twhy <code>heads.edn</code> keeps the whole signed trace.</td></tr>\n   696\t<tr><td><strong>Many sources per principal</strong></td>\n   697\t<td>A cursor per source; a scheduling policy for which candidate loads\n   698\twhen sources disagree.</td>\n   699\t<td>This document 5.5 \"The candidate\" and section 7; the follower's\n   700\tprocess state and the shape <code>heads</code> answers.</td>\n   701\t<td><strong>No</strong> for the trace, <code>judge</code>, the floor\n   702\tand <code>heads.edn</code>. <strong>Yes for one plumbing rule:</strong>\n   703\t\"latest observed wins\" is sound only for one writer's ring. Many\n   704\tsources need \"greatest verified sequence, with a fair turn for an\n   705\tunloadable one\", which is where the two scheduling defects of the\n   706\tsecond review live. That design starts from those findings.</td></tr>\n   707\t<tr><td><strong>UDP on the DHT socket</strong></td>\n   708\t<td>A mirror on the node's datagram socket; a return-path proof.</td>\n   709\t<td><code>dao.jing.dht.md</code> 3 and 8 (frozen);\n   710\t<code>dao.stream.remote.md</code> 3.2; <code>dao.stream.udp</code>:\n   711\tthe proof must ride the outer fragment envelope and be checked before\n   712\treassembly, because a fragment carries no request key\n   713\t(<code>src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:135-149, 312-325</code>);\n   714\t<code>dao.space.dht</code>.</td>\n   715\t<td><strong>No.</strong> It is the costliest step and touches the core\n   716\tnowhere.</td></tr>\n   717\t</table>\n   718\t\n   719\t**The redesign risks, found now.** Three:\n   720\t\n   721\t1. **Identity.** Revision 2 said the name-against-identity question\n   722\t   blocked relaying only. That was wrong: a fixed name as an identity is\n   723\t   already false across one publisher's restart. It is settled in the\n   724\t   core (C3, 5.1) and no later step reopens it.\n   725\t2. The single-candidate rule does not generalise to many sources, and\n   726\t   it gives no progress guarantee under replay even with one (5.5). It\n   727\t   is plumbing, labelled as such; a scheduling policy replaces it\n   728\t   without touching the trace, `judge`, the floor or `heads.edn`.\n   729\t3. **The token assumes one host and one port number for both sockets.**\n   730\t   That needs two independent binds to succeed, and off loopback two\n   731\t   forwarded ports. A third token part of the form `ws:<port>` is\n   732\t   reserved for a differing port number; it is additive. It does\n   733\t   **not** cover a board advertised at another host, a reverse proxy\n   734\t   that changes the path, or two peers that are both unreachable. Each\n   735\t   of those needs the token to carry a full ws address, or a relay\n   736\t   pair; the step off loopback decides the grammar, and the trace does\n   737\t   not depend on it.\n   738\t\n   739\t### 8.3 The first cross-machine transport: WebSocket\n   740\t\n   741\t<table>\n   742\t<tr><th></th><th><code>dao.stream.ws</code></th>\n   743\t<th>UDP on the DHT socket, plus a return-path proof</th></tr>\n   744\t<tr><td>Reflection of traffic at a spoofed source</td>\n   745\t<td>None: a WebSocket rides TCP, whose handshake is the return-path\n   746\tproof.</td>\n   747\t<td>The problem itself. Needs a new proof, on fragments too.</td></tr>\n   748\t<tr><td>Serving an arbitrary ring with existing code</td>\n   749\t<td><strong>Yes.</strong> <code>ws-project/make-acceptor</code> takes\n   750\tany mirror table and <code>accept-step!</code> runs\n   751\t<code>remote/mirror-step</code> per accepted connection\n   752\t(<code>ws_project.cljc:161-190, 248-276</code>);\n   753\t<code>dial</code>, <code>dial-attach!</code> and\n   754\t<code>dial-step!</code> give the reader a reflection\n   755\t(<code>305-420</code>). <code>yin.repl.serve</code> does exactly this\n   756\ttoday with a two-entry table (<code>serve.cljc:224-225, 291</code>).\n   757\t</td>\n   758\t<td>No. The node has no mirror on its socket; that is new code in\n   759\t<code>dao.space.dht</code>.</td></tr>\n   760\t<tr><td>Contracts amended</td>\n   761\t<td>None beyond the core's own name resolution (5.1), which either\n   762\ttransport needs.</td>\n   763\t<td>Two, one of them frozen.</td></tr>\n   764\t<tr><td>NAT</td>\n   765\t<td>One side must accept a TCP connection: a public address or a\n   766\tforwarded port. No hole punching. Either side may be the one that\n   767\tdials, so no node is privileged, but two peers that are both behind\n   768\tNATs need a relay pair\n   769\t(<code>dao.stream.remote.md</code> 3.3, 4).</td>\n   770\t<td>Hole punching is possible in principle, through a meeting peer.\n   771\tThe DHT has none today (<code>dao.jing.dht.md</code> section 6: \"NAT\n   772\tmeeting is not in this epic\").</td></tr>\n   773\t<tr><td>What the reader must already reach</td>\n   774\t<td colspan=\"2\">The publisher's UDP socket, to fetch blobs. With no NAT\n   775\tmeeting in the DHT, cross-machine use today already needs the publisher\n   776\tdirectly reachable. WebSocket asks for the same host on TCP as\n   777\twell.</td></tr>\n   778\t<tr><td>Token</td>\n   779\t<td><code>yin:&lt;host:port&gt;/&lt;principal&gt;</code>, the port\n   780\tnumber shared (5.1); <code>ws:&lt;port&gt;</code> reserved.</td>\n   781\t<td>The same two parts, one socket.</td></tr>\n   782\t<tr><td>Confidentiality</td>\n   783\t<td>None: <code>wss://</code> \"has no settled descriptor form\"\n   784\t(<code>src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:259</code>). The head is public\n   785\tand its integrity is the signature's.</td>\n   786\t<td>None either.</td></tr>\n   787\t</table>\n   788\t\n   789\t**Recommendation: WebSocket, for the core and for the first step off\n   790\tloopback.** It is the transport with a return path, it serves the ring\n   791\twith code that exists, and it amends no contract. UDP on the DHT socket\n   792\tstays the deferred alternative, wanted when hole-punched peers must\n   793\tfollow each other without a relay.\n   794\t\n   795\t**What that step is.** Remove the loopback condition of 5.1, and land\n   796\tthe checks below. No design beyond this section is expected.\n   797\t\n   798\t**Unverified, and to be verified by that step before it lands:**\n   799\t\n   800\t- That the acceptor bounds concurrent sessions. The handoff has 8\n   801\t  slots; whether `:sessions` is bounded under many held connections was\n   802\t  not checked.\n   803\t- That one connection cannot make a step long: `mirror-step` loops to\n   804\t  `blocked`, and the spec's \"composition budget\" was not found in it.\n   805\t- That the listener seams, written for `yin.repl.host`, compose outside\n   806\t  `yin.repl.serve` on all three hosts without moving code.\n   807\t- That a TCP listener binds at the UDP socket's number on each host,\n   808\t  including after an ephemeral UDP bind.\n   809\t\n   810\t### 8.4 The assumption cross-machine adds: locality is not trust\n   811\t\n   812\tOn loopback, whoever connects is on the machine. Off it, anyone is.\n   813\tNothing in the core relied on the first:\n   814\t\n   815\t- **Reading the board** was never restricted. A head is public wherever\n   816\t  its node is reachable, as its index is under `--dht-publish`.\n   817\t- **A hostile or intercepted source** can do four things: show a\n   818\t  forged trace (`:bad-proof`); show one below the floor (`:stale`);\n   819\t  show nothing; or alternate signed traces above the floor so that no\n   820\t  load completes (5.5). The first two are refused. The last two deny\n   821\t  progress and nothing else: no wrong head is installed and the floor\n   822\t  never moves down. `judge` treats every source that way; it has no\n   823\t  notion of a local one.\n   824\t- **First contact has no floor**, on loopback or off. Off loopback an\n   825\t  interceptor on a cleartext channel can show a first-time reader an\n   826\t  old valid head, or withhold. That limit was always in section 10; the\n   827\t  network makes it reachable. `wss` or a second source narrows it;\n   828\t  neither is in the core.\n   829\t- **The index** is fetched by content address and confirmed against the\n   830\t  trace.\n   831\t- **What does change** is resource exposure: connections, sessions,\n   832\t  step time. Those are the unverified items of 8.3, and they are the\n   833\t  whole of the work.\n   834\t\n   835\t## 9. What is rejected\n   836\t\n   837\t- **A head under a DHT key.** C2; it is the mutable-pointer service.\n   838\t- **The signature in `m`.** C1.\n   839\t- **UDP on the DHT socket first.** 8.3.\n   840\t- **A candidate set.** With one source it buys nothing and its capacity\n   841\t  is a way to starve a true head.\n   842\t- **A floor that includes unconfirmed claims.** A replayed, validly\n   843\t  signed, wrong trace would shut out every true head below it.\n   844\t- **Install, then persist.** A reader that used head 20 could restart\n   845\t  at 19.\n   846\t- **Load holders in `dao.space.dht`** (revision 1). A distinct load\n   847\t  kind keeps candidates out of the snapshot set with no new bookkeeping,\n   848\t  and `forget` keeps its contract.\n   849\t- **Removing hydration, and `--dht-recover`** (revision 1). 5.8.\n   850\t- **The board on the REPL endpoint's table.** It would publish the\n   851\t  evaluation service with it.\n   852\t- **The board name as a mirror identity** (revisions 0 and 2), and\n   853\t  **amending `dao.stream.md` to make a service name a legal identity.**\n   854\t  The second would need cursor semantics for a name whose stream is\n   855\t  replaced under it, in the governing contract, to save one request.\n   856\t  Resolution keeps that contract as it is.\n   857\t- **A head operation on `dao.stream.apply`, a DHT wire op, a hash\n   858\t  chain, push.** As before: each adds a mechanism where a ring served as\n   859\t  itself needs none.\n   860\t\n   861\t## 10. Failure modes\n   862\t\n   863\t<table>\n   864\t<tr><th>Event</th><th>What the reader does</th><th>Limit</th></tr>\n   865\t<tr><td>A forged trace</td>\n   866\t<td><code>:yin.head/bad-proof</code>.</td><td>-</td></tr>\n   867\t<tr><td>An old trace shown again</td>\n   868\t<td><code>:yin.head/stale</code> against the durable floor.</td>\n   869\t<td><strong>First contact has no floor</strong>: an old valid head is\n   870\tinstalled. No trace says how new it is.</td></tr>\n   871\t<tr><td>A signed trace whose sequence is wrong</td>\n   872\t<td>Loaded, <code>:yin.head/seq-mismatch</code>, dropped, remembered.\n   873\tThe floor does not move; its index is never resolvable.</td>\n   874\t<td>Only the last rejected trace is remembered. Shown again after\n   875\tanother trace, it is walked again, locally.</td></tr>\n   876\t<tr><td>Signed traces above the floor shown in alternation</td>\n   877\t<td>Each turn releases one load and starts the other. A load that\n   878\tcompletes installs and raises the floor.</td>\n   879\t<td><strong>No progress guarantee</strong>: if no load completes\n   880\twithin a turn, no head is installed while it lasts (5.5). Bounded\n   881\twork; no wrong head.</td></tr>\n   882\t<tr><td>A publisher that rewinds</td>\n   883\t<td>A lower <code>t</code> is <code>:stale</code>.</td>\n   884\t<td>A key holder can sign a new index that omits anything. Declaring a\n   885\tprincipal is trusting it for its own head.</td></tr>\n   886\t<tr><td>Two manifests at one sequence</td>\n   887\t<td><code>:yin.head/equivocation</code>; the installed head stays.</td>\n   888\t<td>A greater sequence ends it.</td></tr>\n   889\t<tr><td>The source shows nothing</td>\n   890\t<td>Nothing. The installed head stays.</td>\n   891\t<td><strong>Withholding is undetectable</strong>: silence and \"no new\n   892\thead\" look the same, and no timeout may tell them apart.</td></tr>\n   893\t<tr><td>A manifest nobody holds</td>\n   894\t<td><code>:yin.head/unloadable</code>; retried at the bounded delay;\n   895\tthe next observed trace replaces it at once.</td><td>-</td></tr>\n   896\t<tr><td>An older load never finishes</td>\n   897\t<td>Abandoned the moment a newer trace is observed; the newer one\n   898\tloads at once (5.5).</td><td>-</td></tr>\n   899\t<tr><td>A crash while installing</td>\n   900\t<td>Before the write: the previous head. After: the new one.</td>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
